# Who guards the guardians when the guardians rotate?

Umpire rotation and inherited integrity in an evolutionary model of institutional corruption.

This repository (1) reproduces the corruption model of Lee, Iwasa, Dieckmann and Sigmund,
"Social evolution leads to persistent corruption", PNAS 116:13276-13281 (2019),
doi:[10.1073/pnas.1900078116](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1900078116), and (2) extends it with
term-limited umpires (rotation) whose newcomers may inherit the integrity of their predecessors.
It contains the code, all numeric results, figures, a first-draft paper in two layouts
(`paper/main.pdf`, single-column preprint; `paper/ieee/main_ieee.pdf`, IEEE two-column journal
format with IEEEtran) and notes for the author.

> **SI verification.** The SI Appendix of Lee et al. (2019) was read in full on the branch
> `si-verification` ([SI_EXTRACT.md](SI_EXTRACT.md), [CHANGES_SI.md](CHANGES_SI.md)). It does not
> describe the individual-based update procedure (update order, time unit, finite-population
> payoffs). Every rule it does state is satisfied by our model, and its Fig. 8 supports our time
> unit quantitatively, so the simulated process did not change. The SI corrected our derivation of
> the rare-exploration matrix (discrepancy D5 withdrawn). The update order and role-model sampling
> remain documented assumptions ([SPEC.md](SPEC.md) Section 8).

## Question

In the model of Lee et al., players play a donation game supervised by umpires who are either
honest (they fine defectors) or corrupt (they take bribes). Players can pay a cost h to check
their umpire. Social learning produces cycles: honest periods let checking lapse, corruption
spreads, cooperation collapses, prudent checking returns, and honesty recovers.
We ask: what happens when umpires serve limited terms and are replaced by newcomers? Does rotation
keep institutions honest, does it remove the cycles, and what if newcomers inherit the integrity
of the body they join (linearly, by conformity, or with a delay)?

## Main results

All numbers below are generated from `results/full/` (see `results/full/numbers.md`). "Mean field"
means the deterministic large-population equations; "IBM" means the stochastic individual-based
model. The two are reported separately.

**Reproduction (Lee et al. 2019)**

| Benchmark | Paper | This repository | Status |
|---|---|---|---|
| B1: all umpires corrupt, OD -> h/(f+B) | 0.25, PC 0.75 | {{BoneOD}}, {{BonePC}} (max deviation {{BoneDev}} over {{BoneStarts}} starts) | reproduced |
| B2: all umpires honest, OC -> 1 | 1 | 1 (max deviation {{BtwoDev}}) | reproduced |
| B3: rare-exploration H:C ratio, N = 10 | (19N+87):109 = 2.54 | {{MarkovRatio}}, derived independently and symbolically | reproduced |
| B3: simulated H:C ratio | about {{PaperRatio}} | ratio of mean shares {{BthreeRatio}} [{{BthreeRatioLo}}, {{BthreeRatioHi}}]; time all honest / all corrupt {{BthreePure}} [{{BthreePureLo}}, {{BthreePureHi}}] ({{Nreps}} runs of {{BthreeGens}} generations) | matched under the Markov-type definition; not under the ratio of mean shares |
| B4: sustained cycles (M = 50, N = 10) | about 7 episodes per 10^5 steps (read from Fig. 1) | {{EpisodesSteps}} [{{EpisodesStepsLo}}, {{EpisodesStepsHi}}] | reproduced |
| B4: damped oscillations (M = 5000, N = 1000) | corrupt fraction about 0.5-0.55 (Fig. 1D) | {{BfourCorrupt}} [{{BfourCorruptLo}}, {{BfourCorruptHi}}]; mean-field focus, eigenvalues {{FermiRe}} +- {{FermiIm}}i | reproduced |
| B4: orbit shrinkage with exploration | qualitative (Fig. 3) | Hopf point at mu = nu = {{HopfMuA}} (A = 2), {{HopfMuB}} (A = 0.7) | reproduced |

The simulated ratio moves toward the Markov value as exploration becomes rarer:
{{BthreeRatioB}} at mu = 3e-4 and {{BthreeRatioC}} at mu = 1e-4. At mu = 1e-3 the result depends
on how the time average is defined: the time with all umpires honest over the time with all
umpires corrupt (what the Markov chain counts) agrees with the published 2.15, the ratio of mean
shares does not ({{LongRunMean}} even in runs of {{LongRunSteps}} steps). The SI does not say which
definition was used. Other candidate causes (burn-in, imitation strength, exploration and payoff
conventions) were checked and do not explain the difference ([CHANGES_SI.md](CHANGES_SI.md)).

**Extension (new; author's design)**

1. *Rotation removes corruption cycles through a saddle-node on invariant circle (mean field).*
   At the default exploration rate (mu = nu = {{MuMF}}) there is no Hopf bifurcation in the
   rotation rate rho. A stable honest equilibrium appears in a fold at rho_c = {{RhoC}} that lies
   on the limit cycle; the period diverges with exponent {{SnicSlope}} (theory: -1/2). A
   small-exploration formula gives rho_c = {{RhoCApprox}} ({{RhoCErr}}% error) and shows that
   rho_c is proportional to the exploration rate (rho_c / mu = {{RhoCOverMuA}} for A = 2).
2. *H1 supported.* Rotation raises time-averaged honesty and damps cycles: IBM small population
   {{HoneYSZero}} -> {{HoneYSMax}} (rho = 0 -> {{HoneRhoMaxIBM}} per generation), corruption
   episodes {{HoneEpSZero}} -> {{HoneEpSMax}} per 1000 generations.
3. *H2 (vigilance paradox) not supported.* Rotation does lower vigilance (prudent cooperators
   {{HtwoPCSZero}} -> {{HtwoPCSThirty}}), but the remaining corruption episodes are shallower and
   shorter, not deeper (lowest cooperation in an episode {{HtwoDCSZero}} -> {{HtwoDCSTen}};
   duration {{HtwoRecSZero}} -> {{HtwoRecSThirty}} generations).
4. *H3 supported.* Inheritance weakens rotation in all three variants. Linear inheritance is
   exactly fresh rotation at rho (1 - kappa) in the mean field. In the IBM the equivalence fails
   the strict pre-registered test at {{HthreeNexcl}} of {{HthreeNtests}} points (largest
   difference {{HthreeMaxDiff}}) and {{HthreeBonf}} after Bonferroni adjustment.
5. *H4: substitutes.* Rotation helps honesty more when transparency is costly (gain at
   rho = 0.05: {{GainLowH}} at h = 0.02 vs {{GainHighH}} at h = 0.28). For cooperation the
   pattern is mixed.
6. *H5 mixed.* Conformist inheritance produces bistability only when newcomers almost purely
   copy the majority (kappa >= {{HfiveKminDefault}} at default parameters) and rotation is fast.
   The pre-registered hysteresis sweep shows no loop; a post-hoc sweep from the corrupt state does.
   The IBM shows no dependence on the starting state.
7. *H6 not supported.* Delayed inheritance never destabilized the honest equilibrium
   ({{HsixPoints}} parameter points; rightmost root real part at most {{HsixMaxRe}}).
8. *Robustness.* Replacing a whole cohort at once (pulses) gives more honesty than continuous
   rotation at the same average rate (IBM: {{PulseIBMCont}} -> {{PulseIBMFull}}). Newcomers who are
   not reliably honest (p0 = 0.6) can make moderate rotation lower honesty (minimum {{PZeroSixtyMin}}).

Note on novelty: in the mean field, fresh-cohort rotation is algebraically the same as umpire
exploration biased toward honesty (DECISIONS.md D-015). Related ideas (enforcer "education",
higher enforcer exploration) appear in Lee et al. (2015) and Zhou et al. (2023). To our knowledge
the bifurcation structure and the inheritance variants have not been studied in this model.

## Repository layout

```
src/            model code (payoffs, ODE, DDE, IBM, Markov chain, rotation, analysis, metrics)
experiments/    digitize_si.py, run_reproduction.py, run_extension.py, run_sensitivity.py,
                evaluate_hypotheses.py, make_figures.py, make_numbers.py,
                make_changes_si.py, render_docs.py
tests/          pytest suite (payoffs, fixed points, IBM vs ODE, Markov chain, rotation limits,
                rho_eff equivalence, rules stated in the SI)
configs/        base.yaml, quick.yaml, full.yaml
results/        JSON outputs with config hash and git commit (full/ and quick/)
figures/        PNG and vector PDF (figures/quick/ for the quick profile)
paper/          main.tex, references.bib, autogen_numbers.tex, main.pdf
  ieee/         main_ieee.tex (IEEEtran journal), refs_ieee.bib (generated), main_ieee.pdf
docs_src/       templates for the Markdown documents (numbers are filled in automatically)
```

Key documents: [SPEC.md](SPEC.md) (model with source locations), [DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md),
[METRICS.md](METRICS.md) (pre-registered metrics and decision rules), [PROGRESS.md](PROGRESS.md),
[NOTES_for_author.md](NOTES_for_author.md), [REVIEW.md](REVIEW.md),
[FINAL_REPORT.md](FINAL_REPORT.md), [CITATIONS_TODO.md](CITATIONS_TODO.md),
[SI_EXTRACT.md](SI_EXTRACT.md) (what the SI specifies), [CHANGES_SI.md](CHANGES_SI.md) (effect of
the SI verification).

## How to run

Requirements: Python 3.12 (3.11 should work), the packages in `requirements.txt` or
`environment.yml`, and a TeX distribution for the paper. Put the reference PDFs in `refs/`
(not distributed).

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
python -m pytest
make all                     # on Windows: mingw32-make all
python run_all.py full       # same pipeline without make
make quick                   # fast check with the quick profile
```

Recorded compute: about {{RuntimeFullMin}} minutes for the full profile and {{RuntimeQuickMin}} minutes for
the quick profile on a 16-thread laptop (14 worker processes).
Individual steps: `python experiments/digitize_si.py full` (needs the SI PDF in refs/),
`python experiments/run_reproduction.py full`,
`python experiments/run_extension.py full [bif e1 e2 e3 e4 e5 e5b e6]`,
`python experiments/run_sensitivity.py full`, `python experiments/evaluate_hypotheses.py full`,
`python experiments/make_figures.py full`, `python experiments/make_numbers.py full`,
`python experiments/make_changes_si.py`, `python experiments/render_docs.py`,
`python experiments/build_paper.py`.

## Reproduction notes: discrepancies and resolved typos

| ID | Where | Issue | Resolution |
|---|---|---|---|
| D1 | Methods p.13281 | Q(y) printed as y1 Q_C + (1 - y1) Q_H although y1 is the honest fraction (also in the PDF, not only PMC) | Q(y) = y1 Q_H + (1 - y1) Q_C; the printed form fails B1 (test) |
| D2 | Methods p.13281 | Honest mixed state called "OD+PD (a mixture of OD and PC)" | It is OD and PD (Fig. 2 caption, Q_H) |
| D3 | Fig. 3 caption | "8 or 9, respectively" is reversed relative to the figure (the SI, p.1, agrees with the figure) | With A = 2 the orbit passes state 9 (distance {{SlowDistNine}}), with A = 0.7 state 8 (distance {{SlowBDistEight}}); explained by the invasion thresholds y_PD = {{yPD}}, y_OD = {{yODA}} (A = 2) or {{yODB}} (A = 0.7) |
| D4 | Transition matrix T | Row 3 omits allOD (H) -> allOD (C), although the SI text (p.2) says corrupt umpires invade this state | State 3 is transient; stationary distribution unchanged (symbolic check). Confirmed by the SI |
| D5 | (withdrawn) | We had called the mu/6 entry in row 4 inconsistent | The SI (p.2) explains it: a neutral dissident fixes with probability 1/2 when two copies already do better. Our derivation now uses this rule |
| D6 | Fig. 3C, D | Caption says the orange orbit (mu = nu = 1e-4) is shown; its period is {{PerSlow}} time units | The panels match the mu = nu = 0.02 orbit (period {{PerViolet}}; peaks OC {{VioletOC}}, PC {{VioletPC}}, PD {{VioletPD}}, OD {{VioletOD}}) |
| D7 | SI Fig. 9 | Axis labels swapped relative to the caption | The caption is right (ranges, Hopf boundary and periods agree with our model) |
| U1-U4 | SI Appendix | Read in full ([SI_EXTRACT.md](SI_EXTRACT.md)) | Exploration rules verified; time unit supported by SI Fig. 8; update order and role-model sampling not stated (assumptions kept); T derivation verified; averaging behind 2.15 not stated |
| PMC | Web version | Minus signs lost | All signs read from the PDF at 300 dpi and checked symbolically |

